WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.725
How do living systems learn so much from so little,

00:00:04.805 --> 00:00:09.030
adapting with sparse data and minimal energy while
artificial deep networks demand massive

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:13.335
datasets and constant retraining?

00:00:13.415 --> 00:00:17.640
This work by Aisha Belhadi at the Okinawa Institute
of Science and Technology looks to biology for answers.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:21.945
It distills three mechanisms of biological
adaptation metaplasticity, homeostasis,

00:00:22.025 --> 00:00:26.250
and inhibition

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:30.555
and proposes concrete ways to fold them into the
Hopfield-network self-optimization model,

00:00:30.635 --> 00:00:34.860
aiming for deep learning tools that learn the way
living systems do.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:40.361
A large part of recent AI progress comes from deep
learning paired with big data,

00:00:40.441 --> 00:00:43.378
trained by supervised or reinforcement signals.

00:00:43.458 --> 00:00:48.579
These systems are powerful, but they behave very
differently from living systems,

00:00:48.659 --> 00:00:53.844
which learn, make associations, and adapt using
sparse data, efficient energy use,

00:00:53.924 --> 00:00:55.642
and few training iterations.

00:00:55.722 --> 00:00:59.366
They also forget previous tasks when trained sequentially,

00:00:59.446 --> 00:01:02.127
a problem known as catastrophic forgetting.

00:01:02.207 --> 00:01:06.749
Hopfield networks, with unsupervised learning and
an associative memory,

00:01:06.829 --> 00:01:09.831
offer a more biologically plausible alternative,

00:01:09.911 --> 00:01:12.207
but their simplest forms are limited,

00:01:12.287 --> 00:01:17.151
storing only about zero point one four times the
number of nodes in patterns,

00:01:17.231 --> 00:01:21.260
and rarely exploit the biological mechanisms that
inspired them.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:25.368
To adapt in an uncertain, changing world,

00:01:25.448 --> 00:01:29.177
complex systems must integrate new information
while maintaining stability.

00:01:29.257 --> 00:01:32.985
Too much destabilization prevents consistent learning;

00:01:33.065 --> 00:01:36.793
too much reliance on past memory locks in outdated
patterns.

00:01:36.873 --> 00:01:40.602
Biology solves this with an interplay of mechanisms

00:01:40.682 --> 00:01:44.410
metaplasticity that tunes how much synapses change,

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:48.218
homeostasis that regulates the system around a set
point, inhibition,

00:01:48.298 --> 00:01:52.027
and resets that enable forgetting.

00:01:52.107 --> 00:01:55.835
The self-optimization model is a Hopfield network
with Hebbian learning and periodic resets

00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:59.643
that already builds a memory of visited attractors
and enlarges the basin of the global

00:01:59.723 --> 00:02:03.452
energy minimum,

00:02:03.532 --> 00:02:07.260
making it a promising candidate to be enriched with
more of these biological features.

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:11.492
This work makes two linked contributions.

00:02:11.572 --> 00:02:15.424
First, it distills key mechanisms of adaptation in
biological systems,

00:02:15.504 --> 00:02:19.355
with a special focus on metaplasticity and
homeostatic regulation,

00:02:19.435 --> 00:02:23.287
plus inhibition and forgetting.

00:02:23.367 --> 00:02:27.219
Second,

00:02:27.299 --> 00:02:31.151
it proposes concrete avenues to incorporate these
features into the self-optimization

00:02:31.231 --> 00:02:35.083
model and Hopfield networks more broadly

00:02:35.163 --> 00:02:39.015
by making the learning rate adaptive with respect
to synaptic weights or the energy landscape,

00:02:39.095 --> 00:02:42.946
by enhancing modular structure, and by changing the
activation rule.

00:02:43.026 --> 00:02:46.878
The aim is deep learning tools that recapitulate
the advantages of biological systems

00:02:46.958 --> 00:02:50.810
and can plausibly model living and adaptive systems
across levels of complexity.

00:02:51.190 --> 00:02:56.663
The core idea is to raise the network's degrees of
freedom without adding new parameters,

00:02:56.743 --> 00:03:00.468
by letting existing components do more work, as
biology does.

00:03:00.548 --> 00:03:02.028
For local metaplasticity,

00:03:02.108 --> 00:03:07.830
the learning rate alpha is no longer a user-defined
constant but a function of the connection

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:09.390
weight between two nodes,

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:14.880
so the Hebbian update becomes weight-dependent: the
change in a weight equals a function

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:18.374
of that weight times the product of the two node states.

00:03:18.454 --> 00:03:23.802
This can potentiate co-activated synapses faster
and depress opposing ones more slowly.

00:03:23.882 --> 00:03:25.861
For network-level metaplasticity,

00:03:25.941 --> 00:03:29.604
alpha is instead tied to the change in the
network's energy,

00:03:29.684 --> 00:03:35.281
so learning slows in shallow parts of the energy
landscape and speeds up on steeper slopes,

00:03:35.361 --> 00:03:36.966
helping avoid local minima.

00:03:37.046 --> 00:03:40.959
Homeostasis is approximated by oscillatory
activation functions,

00:03:41.039 --> 00:03:46.262
such as trigonometric ones, and adaptivity is
further supported by modular structure,

00:03:46.342 --> 00:03:52.313
where modules that consistently strengthen their
internal connections receive an automatic boost,

00:03:52.393 --> 00:03:55.620
and inter-module inhibition regulates overall energy.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:00.887
This paper is a conceptual and position piece
rather than an empirical study,

00:04:00.967 --> 00:04:04.306
so it does not introduce a dataset or run benchmarks.

00:04:04.386 --> 00:04:09.595
Instead, it builds on existing computational work
with the self-optimization model

00:04:09.675 --> 00:04:15.659
including a model of the C. elegans connectome that
used inhibitory inter-cluster connections,

00:04:15.739 --> 00:04:19.723
and SO models that solve combinatorial
satisfiability problems.

00:04:19.803 --> 00:04:25.400
These prior systems define the setting in which the
proposed biological mechanisms would

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:27.980
eventually be implemented and evaluated.

00:04:28.360 --> 00:04:30.373
Because this is a proposal paper,

00:04:30.453 --> 00:04:33.670
its main result is conceptual rather than numerical.

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:38.997
It delivers a unified framework showing how
metaplasticity, homeostasis, inhibition,

00:04:39.077 --> 00:04:44.071
and forgetting can each map onto specific changes
in the self-optimization model

00:04:44.151 --> 00:04:47.495
an adaptive learning rate driven by weights or energy,

00:04:47.575 --> 00:04:52.188
oscillatory activation functions, and adaptive
modularity with inhibition.

00:04:52.268 --> 00:04:57.642
The unifying insight is that constraints and
degrees of freedom are not in opposition:

00:04:57.722 --> 00:05:01.510
the network architecture provides the constraining
framework,

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:06.774
and within it, higher degrees of freedom can expand
the space of adaptive behavior.

00:05:06.854 --> 00:05:11.720
Without those constraints, the added freedom would
simply collapse into noise.

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:16.695
There are no ablation experiments in this work,
since no model is trained.

00:05:16.775 --> 00:05:18.211
The paper does, however,

00:05:18.291 --> 00:05:22.444
sketch the variations that a future empirical study
should compare.

00:05:22.524 --> 00:05:29.204
These include different forms of the function that
links the learning rate to weights or to energy changes,

00:05:29.284 --> 00:05:33.817
ranging from simple linear approximations to more
complex nonlinear ones,

00:05:33.897 --> 00:05:38.365
and different activation functions such as sigmoid,
Heaviside threshold,

00:05:38.445 --> 00:05:40.134
and trigonometric functions.

00:05:40.214 --> 00:05:45.568
The author suggests these variations could
reproduce different normal and pathological

00:05:45.648 --> 00:05:48.032
behavioral modes in biological systems,

00:05:48.112 --> 00:05:52.770
and are worth exploring systematically once the
mechanisms are implemented.

00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:58.355
The one quantitative anchor in this paper is the
classic capacity limit of a standard

00:05:58.435 --> 00:06:03.764
Hopfield network: it can reliably store only about
zero point one four times the number

00:06:03.844 --> 00:06:06.998
of nodes in the network in distinct memory patterns.

00:06:07.078 --> 00:06:13.651
This ceiling is a key reason the paper looks to
biological mechanisms to extend the network's capabilities.

00:06:13.731 --> 00:06:17.443
Beyond that figure, the work reports no
experimental numbers,

00:06:17.523 --> 00:06:22.355
because it is a conceptual proposal that
synthesizes ideas across neuroscience,

00:06:22.435 --> 00:06:26.770
molecular biology, and machine learning rather than
measuring a system.

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:30.963
The takeaway is that the mechanisms biology uses to
adapt metaplasticity,

00:06:31.043 --> 00:06:34.855
homeostasis, and inhibition

00:06:34.935 --> 00:06:38.748
can be translated into machine learning by
adjusting the learning rate according to synaptic

00:06:38.828 --> 00:06:42.641
weights or the system's energy,

00:06:42.721 --> 00:06:46.534
by enhancing modular structure,

00:06:46.614 --> 00:06:50.426
and by modifying the activation rule of a
Hopfield-network self-optimization model.

00:06:50.506 --> 00:06:54.319
Doing so could yield associative-memory systems
capable of complex adaptive learning from sparse data,

00:06:54.399 --> 00:06:58.212
closer to how living systems continually adjust and
thrive.

00:06:58.292 --> 00:07:02.105
The broader message is that constraints and
flexibility work together: the right constraints

00:07:02.185 --> 00:07:05.997
let increased degrees of freedom open up new
adaptive possibilities instead of collapsing

00:07:06.077 --> 00:07:09.890
into noise.
